---
/**
 * Privacy.
 *
 * Owner: trust-compliance-officer (docs/agents/ROSTER.md §3). Rewritten for this session, not
 * appended to: the previous revision was an interim baseline, and a privacy page that accumulates
 * paragraphs stops describing the product it is attached to.
 *
 * Every statement is verified against the repository at the reviewed date, not against a plan:
 * apps/web is fully pre-rendered and ships no hydrated island; no page in apps/web/src contains a
 * form, a cookie, a fetch, an analytics tag, an ad script, or a third-party resource; the typeface
 * is self-hosted in apps/web/public/fonts; apps/web/public/_headers serves a
 * Content-Security-Policy of `default-src 'self'` with no third-party origin allowed; and no
 * localStorage key is written anywhere in apps/** or packages/** at the reviewed date.
 *
 * The browser-storage section states a BOUND rather than a snapshot — the theme preference is
 * the only value DelayPilot may ever write, it is written only on an explicit choice, and it
 * never leaves the device — so the sentence stays true whether or not the theme control has
 * shipped. If anything else is ever written to a reader's browser, this page is wrong and must
 * be rewritten in the same change.
 *
 * What this page must never do is state a retention period, a sub-processor, a data-processing
 * agreement, or a legal basis that nothing in the repository establishes. docs/PRIVACY.md (owner:
 * security-privacy-engineer) does not exist yet; when it does, this page renders it and any
 * sentence here that it does not support is a defect in this page, not in that document.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

const title = 'Privacy — DelayPilot'
const description =
  'DelayPilot collects nothing about you today: no account, no cookie, no analytics, no advertising, and no third-party script. What that means, and what is built never to be collected.'
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>

    <p>
      <strong>DelayPilot collects nothing about you today.</strong> There is no account to create, no
      cookie set, no analytics, no advertising, and no third-party script on any page. Reading this site
      sends nothing to DelayPilot beyond the request your browser has to make to fetch the page itself.
      This page says what that means precisely, and what the product is built never to collect once it
      does more than it does today.
    </p>

    <p class="meta">Reviewed <time datetime="2026-09-12">12 September 2026</time>.</p>

    <h2>What is collected today: nothing</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>No account.</strong> There is no sign-up, no sign-in, no password, and no profile.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No form sends anything to DelayPilot.</strong> Every page on this site is a pre-rendered
        file. No DelayPilot application code runs when you request one, and no page posts data to a DelayPilot
        endpoint.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No cookies.</strong> DelayPilot sets none — not a session cookie, not a preference cookie,
        not an analytics cookie. There is no consent banner because there is nothing to consent to.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No analytics.</strong> No analytics product, tag manager, pixel, session recorder, heatmap,
        or experiment framework is loaded. No page view is counted by DelayPilot.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No advertising.</strong> No ad network, no ad script, no ad identifier. See the{' '}
        <a href="/advertising-policy/">advertising policy</a> for the rules that will bind if that ever
        changes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No third-party requests.</strong> No embedded video, no hosted font, no external image,
        no social widget, no error-reporting service. The typeface this page renders in is served from
        this site, so reading DelayPilot does not disclose your visit to a font provider.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What your browser is allowed to load</h2>

    <p>
      The site sends security headers with every page that restrict what the page may do, and they
      are worth stating in plain words because they are a check on us as much as on an attacker:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        Scripts, styles, fonts, images, and network connections may come only from DelayPilot's own
        address. A third-party tracker added by accident would be refused by your browser rather
        than quietly run.
      </li>
      <li>
        Plugin content is blocked outright, and the page may not be embedded in another site's
        frame.
      </li>
      <li>
        Your browser is told not to guess file types, and to trim the address it passes to other
        sites when you follow a link away from here.
      </li>
      <li>
        Geolocation, camera, microphone, and payment interfaces are switched off for these pages.
        DelayPilot never asks for your location.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What is stored in your browser</h2>

    <p>
      DelayPilot sets no cookie. The only value it may ever write to your device is your theme
      preference — <code>system</code>, <code>light</code>, or <code>dark</code> — stored under the key{
        ' '
      }
      <code>dp-theme</code> in your browser's local storage, and written only if you choose a theme. It
      stays on your device: it is not a cookie, it is not an identifier, it is never transmitted, and
      no DelayPilot server can read it. Clearing this site's data in your browser removes it.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you never choose a theme, nothing is written at all. The light or dark appearance of these
      pages otherwise follows the preference your operating system already publishes to your
      browser, which the stylesheet reads directly — never a script, never stored, never sent
      anywhere.
    </p>

    <h2>Looking up a flight</h2>

    <p>
      DelayPilot never asks for a booking reference or record locator, and never will. A flight is
      identified by airline and flight number and date, or by route and date — information that is
      public on a departure board.
    </p>

    <p>
      No flight-data provider is connected to this deployment, so a lookup cannot reach one: it ends
      in the labelled <strong>Unavailable</strong> state rather than returning invented times. These pages
      are pre-rendered files and this deployment has no DelayPilot search endpoint behind them, so what
      you type into a lookup is not transmitted to DelayPilot.
    </p>

    <h2>What DelayPilot is built never to collect</h2>

    <p>
      These are invariants the codebase is written and reviewed against, not preferences that a
      later release might quietly relax:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>No booking reference (PNR or record locator), anywhere.</strong> Not as an input, not
        as a database column, not in a URL, not in a log line, not in an analytics event.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No identity documents.</strong> Never a passport, a government ID, a Known Traveler Number,
        a redress number, a card number, or an airline account password.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No access to your accounts.</strong> DelayPilot never reads an email inbox, never scrapes
        an airline account, and never signs in anywhere as you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nothing filed on your behalf.</strong> No claim, complaint, refund request, rebooking,
        or purchase is ever submitted for you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No personal content in technical records.</strong> Logs, analytics events, error reports,
        page titles, social preview tags, and URLs carry no email address, no name, no itinerary detail,
        no receipt text, no notification content, and no raw IP address.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What the hosting provider sees</h2>

    <p>
      This site is served by a hosting provider, and the hosting provider may keep standard request
      logs under its own policy. DelayPilot does not receive, store, or analyse them. No DelayPilot
      code sits in the path of a page request to add to them, and no DelayPilot record of your visit
      exists anywhere.
    </p>

    <h2>What changes when the product does</h2>

    <p>
      DelayPilot will eventually do things that require data: a saved trip, an email alert, a
      purchase. Each of those brings a new section to this page, and each section is added{' '}
      <strong>when the feature exists</strong> — never in advance, and never as a description of something
      that has not shipped. This page is rewritten rather than added to, so that it never becomes a history
      of intentions.
    </p>

    <p>
      No retention period appears on this page, because nothing is retained. Publishing a number now
      would be inventing one. The rules already binding on that future work are these: an email
      address is stored encrypted and matched by a keyed one-way fingerprint rather than by the
      address itself; analytics, if enabled, records privacy-minimized events that never include an
      email address, a name, a booking reference, an exact itinerary, receipt text, claim content,
      or a raw IP address; operational alerts about your flight stay separate from marketing
      messages, with a one-click unsubscribe for the latter; and export and deletion routes ship
      with the account that makes them meaningful.
    </p>

    <h2>Your choices today</h2>

    <p>
      There is nothing to opt out of, nothing to export, and nothing to delete, because nothing
      about you exists to act on. If you have set a theme, clearing this site's data in your browser
      removes it. That is the whole list, and it is short on purpose.
    </p>

    <h2>Contacting us about this page</h2>

    <p>
      DelayPilot publishes no contact address today. Printing one that nobody monitors would be
      worse than saying so: there is currently no address to write to about this page, and this page
      will name one on the day it exists.
    </p>

    <h2>Related policies</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/terms/">Terms of use</a> — what DelayPilot is, and what it never does for you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/advertising-policy/">Advertising policy</a> — including what is never sent to an advertiser.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">Affiliate disclosure</a> — commercial links, and the absence
        of them.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
